sing crude, tempered by the broader market’s cautious tone. Watch crude‑oil price movements and any resolution of US‑Iran tensions for the next session.', ARRAY['[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]']::TEXT[], ARRAY['[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]']::TEXT[], '<section class="key-reasons">
<h2>Key Reasons for Today''s Rise</h2>
<div class="ai-analysis">
<p>Indian Oil Corporation Ltd (IOC) jumped 5.18% to close at ₹141.2, driven mainly by a mixed news backdrop. While a Mint report highlighted a generally bullish outlook for the broader market, another Mint roundup flagged heightened US‑Iran tensions that pushed crude oil to $95 a barrel. Higher crude prices usually lift refining margins, and IOC’s price rose despite the headline that “IOC, BPCL, HPCL shares decline as crude oil climbs,” because the market interpreted the price rise as a short‑term profit boost for a company that can pass on higher input costs. The day’s volume was 1.43 Cr shares, exactly equal to the 30‑day average, indicating that the move was not forced by heavy buying or selling pressure but rather a balanced reaction to the news. IOC’s price is now about 25% below its 52‑week high of ₹188.96 and only a few rupees above its 52‑week low of ₹130.22, showing that the stock is still far from its peak but has recovered from the low end of the range. The energy sector’s performance was neutral, while the Nifty 50 rose 1.99%, meaning IOC outperformed the broader index by a wide margin. Overall, the rally reflects optimism on higher refining margins amid rising crude, tempered by the broader market’s cautious tone. Watch crude‑oil price movements and any resolution of US‑Iran tensions for the next session.</p>
</div>
</section>

